WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.310
Security, as you can imagine, is incredibly important on Z.

2
00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.310
If I can't keep my data away from hackers, I'm toast.

3
00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.310
Nobody would ever trust my environment with their applications.

4
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:16.310
Security is actually a three-sided system.

5
00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:18.310
There's the confidentiality aspect.

6
00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.310
That part limits the access people have to resources.

7
00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.310
And by resources, that's not just files.

8
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.310
It's also devices, system access, applications,

9
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.310
and every resource needs to be controlled from a security angle.

10
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.310
Then there's availability.

11
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:38.310
Technically, the world's most secure system is one that nobody can even do anything with.

12
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.310
But what's the point of that, right?

13
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.310
I have to make those resources available to the people who should have access to them.

14
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.310
And that's availability.

15
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.310
The third aspect is integrity.

16
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.310
Being able to verify that the data being accessed hasn't been messed around with.

17
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:57.310
This is not only for data-like financial records, but also for applications.

18
00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.310
How can I be sure the program about to run hasn't been altered by a hacker

19
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.310
to move a whole bunch of money from my account into theirs?

20
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:09.310
If you have all three of these things, you've got the makings of a pretty good security model.

21
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:13.310
When a user or a program goes to access something, here's what happens.

22
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.310
And it's kind of important to remember that what it's accessing is a resource.

23
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.310
So that could be a data set.

24
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.310
It could be a disk storage system.

25
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:22.310
It could be a program.

26
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.310
It could be a terminal.

27
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.310
It could be anything.

28
00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.310
So what happens first is the user makes a request.

29
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.310
It says, I want to do this thing.

30
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.310
And it makes a request to the resource manager.

31
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.310
Now, the resource manager is ultimately what makes the connection or doesn't make the connection

32
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.310
between the requesting user and the resource at once to get.

33
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.310
So it needs to make a decision.

34
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:48.310
It's going to make a request to SAP the system authorization facility.

35
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:53.310
That acts as a router, which routes the request to the right system to make that decision.

36
00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.310
In this case, let's just say we're using RACF.

37
00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.310
RACF stands for the resource access control facility.

38
00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:05.310
RACF is going to go through its database and look for any records it has about that resource and that user.

39
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.310
And it can come back and say, yeah, that user can access that.

40
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.310
Or it might come back and say, you know what?

41
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.310
It says right here, they're not supposed to have access to them.

42
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.310
Don't make that connection.

43
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.310
Or it can actually come back and say, you know what?

44
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.310
I don't know anything about either of those things.

45
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.310
So it's up to you resource manager.

46
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:29.310
Ultimately, the resource manager is going to say yes or no to the user and make that connection or not.

47
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:35.310
Something that makes this possible is the 4767 cryptographic co-processor.

48
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:44.310
This is a hardware security module, HSM, that improves security by performing a lot of cryptographic functions on its specialized hardware.

49
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.310
On Z, that's the crypto express.

50
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.310
It's one of those PCIe cards that lives in the I-O section of the frame.

51
00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:58.310
This card supports the federal information processing standard, FIPs, which are the US government security standard.

52
00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:03.310
And it supports security level 4, which is the highest level of security.

53
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:10.310
At this level, the physical security mechanisms provide a complete envelope of protection around the cryptographic module.

54
00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:23.310
So if someone were to pull a heist where they broke into a data center with their masks on and they tiptoe over to the main frame and they gently slide out the HSM, throw it in their bag and jump in the getaway helicopter.

55
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:30.310
The card would detect that it's being tampered with and delete all the sensitive information on it, including those keys.

56
00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:37.310
Hammers, screwdrivers, electrical probes, stethoscopes, they're not getting anything important out of that card.

57
00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:41.310
All of this helps create the most secure computing environment around.
